NAME
    apath - make a path absolute (#include "../lib_apath.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_apath.c"     (includes lib_fs.c)
    char out[APLEN];              APLEN = 128
    used = abspath(out, word);

DESCRIPTION
    abspath(out, a) builds the absolute form of the path word a: the
    current directory (getcwd) + "/" + a when a is relative, then "."
    components dropped and ".." folded into their parent, so
    "/BIN/../MAN/" comes out "/MAN". There is no trailing "/" except on
    the root itself. The word ends at NUL or a space, so a whole
    command tail may be passed; the result is how many characters of a
    were used. Nothing is opened or checked: it only builds a string.

    Y1/OS resolves relative paths itself, so a command needs this only
    where it shows or compares paths: find and grep -r (the printed
    paths), mv (the same file? the same directory?), cp -r (into
    itself?) and the tree walker.

LIMITS
    out must hold APLEN (128) bytes; a longer path is cut.

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: getcwd() for SYS_GETCWD; the . and ..
    folding and the bound are new (the P8X only put the current
    directory in front, because its BIOS resolved every path from the
    root).
    Ported from P8X os/commands/lib_apath.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    fs, walk, mv, cp
